TASK: VehicleMotor waypoint follower + wiring (Phase 3)

ACCEPTANCE
- rotate-then-drive: the car turns in place beyond ALIGN_TOLERANCE (0.08 rad,
  ~one 60fps frame of turning), then drives where its nose points, so it
  cannot crab sideways or cut a corner on an arc.
- constant speed 1.6 u/s, 4.5 rad/s turn, 0.25 arrival radius; a route ends
  parked, and passing a waypoint advances the cursor (which is also what stops
  a fresh route stalling on the tile the car already stands on).
- newest tap wins end to end: a second tap mid-route replaces the path; the
  motor copies the route it is given rather than holding the caller's array.
- unwired modules are now wired in main.ts: pointerdown -> ndcFromPoint ->
  tapAt -> isCurrent -> findPath -> motor.setPath, with rig.setTarget(motor
  .position) following the car.

TEST-FIRST FINDINGS (Red -> Green)
- With a 20-degree alignment tolerance the car left every waypoint on an arc
  (about 1.3cm off the line) and transiently overshot its heading. Tightening
  the tolerance to roughly one frame of turning is what makes "rotate, then
  drive" mean what it says.
- The kit's vehicles are authored facing -z (a truck's taller cargo half sits
  along +z, measured from its own geometry), while town models face +z. Without
  the half turn inside the holder the truck drove cab-last, which is invisible
  in a still frame and obvious the moment it moves.
- A tap outside the town resolved to a ground point in the void and drove the
  car off the map, over ground that is not there. Found by verifying in the
  browser, not in a test: the router now clamps to the grid's footprint, and
  the whole rim is ring road, so an out-of-town tap always ends on a street.
  Two existing tests were tapping outside the 5x5 test town without knowing it
  and had to move.

COVERAGE
- vehicleMotor.ts and vehicleActor.ts both 100% statements/branches/functions
  /lines; 180 tests across 16 files; biome clean, tsc --noEmit clean.

DEFERRED (recorded in the plan)
